{
    "a_galaxy_workflow": "true",
    "annotation": "Building an amplicon sequence variant (ASV) table from 16S data using DADA2",
    "comments": [],
    "creator": [
        {
            "class": "Person",
            "identifier": "l.salgueiro@udc.es",
            "name": "Luc\u00eda Julia Salgueiro Redondo"
        }
    ],
    "format-version": "0.1",
    "name": "ASVtable_withDADA2",
    "readme": "This is a reproducible workflow for processing paired-end 16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing data (V4 region) using Galaxy and DADA2. The pipeline generates Amplicon Sequence Variants (ASVs), assigns taxonomy using the SILVA v138 reference database, and creates a phyloseq object for downstream microbiome analyses.\n\nWORKFLOW SUMMARY: \nNCBI SRA\n   \u2193\nDownload FASTQ (fasterq-dump)\n   \u2193\nFastQC\n   \u2193\nMultiQC\n   \u2193\nFilterAndTrim (DADA2)\n   \u2193\nFastQC\n   \u2193\nMultiQC\n   \u2193\nUnzip Collection\n   \u2193\nlearnErrors (Forward)\n   \u2193\nlearnErrors (Reverse)\n   \u2193\nDADA (Forward)\n   \u2193\nDADA (Reverse)\n   \u2193\nmergePairs\n   \u2193\nmakeSequenceTable\n   \u2193\nremoveBimeraDenovo\n   \u2193\nsequenceCounts\n   \u2193\nassignTaxonomy + addSpecies (SILVA v138)\n   \u2193\nCreate phyloseq object\n   \u2193\nDownstream analyses\n\nINPUT DATA:\n- SRA accession numbers (SRR) obtained from the NCBI SRA Run Selector.\n- Text file containing one SRR accession per line.\n- Sample metadata table.\n\nSOFTWARE AND TOOLS:\n- Galaxy\n- FastQC\n- MultiQC\n- DADA2 Galaxy tools\n- SILVA v138 reference database\n- Phyloseq\n\nSTEP 1. Download Sequencing Reads from SRA\n- Tool: Faster Download and Extract Reads in FASTQ Format from NCBI SRA\n- Input: Text file containing SRR accession numbers.\n- Output: Paired-end FASTQ collection\n\nSTEP 2. Initial Quality Assessment\n- Tool 1: FastQC\nInput: Raw paired-end FASTQ files\nParameters: Default settings\n- Tool 2: MultiQC\nInput: FastQC reports\nParameters: Default settings\nEvaluate: Per-base sequence quality, GC content, Sequence duplication levels, Total number of reads\n\nSTEP 3. Quality Filtering and Trimming\n- Tool: DADA2: FilterAndTrim\n- Input 1: Forward Reads. \nParameters:\ntruncQ: 2, \ntrimLeft: 0,\ntrimRight: 0,\ntruncLen: 240,\nminLen: 20,\nmaxN: 0,\nminQ: 0,\nmaxEE: 2,\nrmPhiX: TRUE\n- Input 2: Reverse Reads\nParameters:\ntruncQ:\t2,\ntrimLeft: 0,\ntrimRight: 0,\ntruncLen: 235,\nminLen: 20,\nmaxN: 0,\nminQ: 0,\nmaxEE: 2,\nrmPhiX: TRUE\n- Output: Filtered and trimmed paired-end read collection, collection with statistics for each sample\n\nSTEP 4. Post-filtering Quality Assessment\n- Tool 1: FastQC\nInput: filtered reads.\nParameters: Default\n- Tool 2: MultiQC\nInput: FastQC reports\nParameters: Default\nEvaluate: verify improvements in overall sequence quality after filtering and trimming.\n\nSTEP 5. Separate Forward and Reverse Reads\n- Tool: Unzip Collection\n- Input: FilterAndTrim output\n- Output: Forward read collection, Reverse read collection\n\nSTEP 7. Learn Error Rates\n- Tool: DADA2: learnErrors\n- Input 1:Forward read collection\n- Output 1:\n1. The error model: error rates data set of dada2, which is a R object\n2. Error rates plot (PDF)\n- Input 2: Reverse read collection\n- Output 2: \n1. The error model: error rates data set of dada2, which is a R object\n2. Error rates plot (PDF)\n- Expected Result: Error rates should decrease as quality scores increase, indicating an appropriate error model fit.\n\nSTEP 8. ASV Inference\n- Tool: DADA2: dada\n- Input 1: Forward read collection, Forward error model\n- Input 2: Reverse read collection, Reverse error model\n- Purpose: Inference of exact Amplicon Sequence Variants (ASVs) through DADA2's statistical error-correction model.\n- Output: a data set of type dada2_dada, which is a RData file containing the output of dada2's dada function\n\nSTEP 9. Merge Paired Reads\n- Tool: DADA2: mergePairs\n- Input: DADA forward output, DADA reverse output, Forward read collection, Reverse read collection\n- Purpose: Merge overlapping forward and reverse reads corresponding to the same DNA fragment.\n- Criteria:\n1. Minimum overlap: 12 bp\n2. No mismatches allowed in the overlap region\n- Output: \n1. A data set of type dada2_mergepairs, which is a RData file containing the output of dada2's mergePairs function\n2. Table with: all the merged sequence variants, sequence abundances, and merging statistics\n\nSTEP 10. Construct the ASV Table\n- Tool: DADA2: makeSequenceTable\n- Input: Merged read data\n- Parameters:\n1. Length filter method: No filter\n2. Plot sequence length distribution: Yes\n- Output: \n1. Graphic with sequence length distribution, \n2. A sequence table: a matrix with rows corresponding to (and named by) the samples, and columns corresponding to (and named by) the sequence variants. The values are ASV abundances\n\nSTEP 11. Chimera Removal\n- Tool: DADA2: removeBimeraDenovo\n- Input: ASV sequence table\n- Purpose: Identify and remove chimeric sequences generated during PCR amplification or sequencing.\n- Output: A data set of type dada2_sequenceTable (chimera-free ASV table)\n\nSTEP 12. Read Retention Summary\n- Tool: DADA2: sequenceCounts\n- Input: filterAndTrim statistics, output of dada2: dada for forward reads, output of dada2: dada for reverse reads, output of dada2: mergePairs, output of dada2: makeSequenceTable, output of dada2: removeBimeraDenovo\n- Output: Summary table tracking the number of reads retained throughout each processing step\n\nSTEP 13. Taxonomic Assignment\n- Tool: DADA2: assignTaxonomy + addSpecies\n- Inputs: Chimera-free ASV sequences, SILVA v138 reference database\n- Parameters: Try reverse complement: Yes. The reverse-complement of each sequence will be used for classification if it is a better match to the reference sequences than the forward sequence (tryRC)\n- Output: Taxonomic classification at multiple ranks: Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species. Unclassified taxa are reported as NA.\n\nSTEP 14. Create a Phyloseq Object\n- Tool: Create phyloseq Object from DADA2\n- Inputs: ASV or sequence table (output of dada2: removeBimeraDenovo), Taxonomy table (output of dada2: assignTaxonomy and addSpecies), Sample metadata\n- Output: A phyloseq object ready for ecological and statistical analyses in R",
    "report": {
        "markdown": "\n# Workflow Execution Report\n\n## Workflow Inputs\n```galaxy\ninvocation_inputs()\n```\n\n## Workflow Outputs\n```galaxy\ninvocation_outputs()\n```\n\n## Workflow\n```galaxy\nworkflow_display()\n```\n"
    },
